"**The answer: a flawed hero of faith. His failures were his own. His strength was God's.** Revision 4: 136 words, about 58 seconds, series HERO OR VILLAIN? Every line and every picture was checked against the ESV by an independent fact-checker, and the last round covered every change in this revision.\n\n| Beat | Time | Voiceover | Reference |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Hook | 0:00\u20130:03 | Was Samson a hero or a villain? | \u2014 |\n| Stakes | 0:03\u20130:06 | Most think his strength was in his hair. | Judges 16:17 |\n| Story | 0:07\u20130:11 | But three times, the Spirit of the Lord rushed upon him. | Judges 14:6, 14:19, 15:14 |\n| Story | 0:11\u20130:13 | His weakness? His eyes. | Judges 14:1, 14:7, 16:1 |\n| Story | 0:13\u20130:20 | He demanded a wife from Israel's enemies. \"Get her for me, for she is right in my eyes.\" | Judges 13:1, 14:1\u20134 |\n| Story | 0:21\u20130:27 | Later, he told Delilah he was set apart to God. No razor, or his strength would leave. | Judges 13:5, 16:4, 16:17 |\n| Story | 0:28\u20130:32 | She had his seven locks shaved. The Lord had left him. | Judges 16:19\u201320 |\n| Story | 0:32\u20130:36 | The man who lived by his eyes lost his eyes. | Judges 16:21 |\n| Turn | 0:37\u20130:42 | But blind, he looked to God for strength, to avenge his two eyes. | Judges 16:21, 16:28 |\n| Turn | 0:42\u20130:44 | And the house fell. | Judges 16:30 |\n| Turn | 0:44\u20130:50 | Hebrews 11 names him in a list. Through faith they \"were made strong out of weakness.\" | Hebrews 11:32\u201334 |\n| Landing | 0:51\u20130:57 | Hero or villain? A flawed hero of faith. His failures were his own. His strength was God's. | Judges 16:1, 16:17, 16:20, 16:28; Hebrews 11:32, 11:33, 11:39 |\n\n### What the viewer learns\n\n1. His strength came from God, not his hair: three times \"the Spirit of the LORD rushed upon him\" (Judges 14:6, 14:19, 15:14).\n2. His uncut hair marked him as set apart to God, and the text never calls it the source of his strength. Samson said a razor would take his strength; when it was shaved, \"the LORD had left him\" (Judges 13:5, 16:17, 16:19\u201320).\n3. The man who lived by his eyes (\"she is right in my eyes\") lost his eyes, and his last prayer was to avenge them (Judges 14:3, 16:21, 16:28).\n4. Hebrews 11 names him with Gideon, Barak and David, among those who through faith \"were made strong out of weakness\" (Hebrews 11:32\u201334).\n\n- **On screen from frame one:** the series tag HERO OR VILLAIN? above the title WAS SAMSON A HERO OR A VILLAIN?\n- **Verse cards:** \"Get her for me, for she is right in my eyes.\" \u2014 Judges 14:3 (ESV), 0:15.9\u20130:20.5 \u00b7 \"were made strong out of weakness\" \u2014 Hebrews 11:34 (ESV), 0:47.7\u20130:50.2\n- **Caption** (posted under the reel):\n    - Hero or villain? The answer is in Judges 13\u201316 and Hebrews 11:32\u201334. Read it yourself.\n    - Three things most people miss: (1) Before he was born, the angel of the LORD said he would \"begin to save Israel from the hand of the Philistines\" (Judges 13:3\u20135). (2) The crowd at the pillars was praising their god Dagon for handing Samson over: \"Our god has given Samson our enemy into our hand\" (Judges 16:23\u201324). (3) The last verse of Judges echoes his words: \"Everyone did what was right in his own eyes\" (Judges 21:25).\n    - Send this to someone who thinks Samson's strength was in his hair.\n"
